WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.060
Hey Wisecrack, Jared again.

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:20.250
If memes are anything to go by, it seems like
the Star Wars prequels have gone through a

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.769
resurgence in popularity since their initial
critical pounding.

00:00:23.769 --> 00:00:27.230
So, that leads us to wonder: were we all wrong?

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:31.010
Did we misjudge what are, in fact, cinema
classics?

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:33.500
"I don't like sand."

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.340
Eh, probably not.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.230
But it wasn’t for a lack of trying.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:40.530
As we talked about in our What Went Wrong
on The Phantom Menace, George Lucas had some

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.070
pretty grand ambitions.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:45.789
Now, in Attack of the Clones, Lucas had epic
ambitions.

00:00:45.789 --> 00:00:50.180
Like, to create an actual epic romance in
the style of classics like ‘Gone With The

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:51.230
Wind’ or ‘Cleopatra.’

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:55.969
He even used one particular romantic movie
from the 1960s as a template.

00:00:55.969 --> 00:00:59.500
But, did Lucas succeed in his ambitions?

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:03.750
Not quite, but if we dive into his original
inspiration, we can discover how the story

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:07.830
of a spoiled brat – “It’s not fair!”
– dating a patronizing princess — “Annie,

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:10.650
you’ll always be that little boy I met on
Tatooine."

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.200
— went so horribly wrong.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.920
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on Star
Wars Episode II: What Went Wrong.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.260
And, of course, spoilers ahead, if you really
care about Star Wars prequel spoilers...

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:24.400
It might have been a while since you last
saw this movie, so here’s a quick recap.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.640
Set over a decade after The Phantom Menace,
Attack of the Clones follows a now sort of

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:33.710
grown up Anakin Skywalker who is charged with
protecting the once Queen, now Senator, Padme

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:36.830
Amidala from multiple assassination attempts.

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:40.270
After staying in a variety of romantic locations,
wouldn’t you know it?

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:41.270
They fall in love.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:45.420
Meanwhile, Obi Wan discovers that an army
of clones has been created for the Republic.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:50.660
From this, he uncovers a separatist plot to
start a civil war led by former Jedi, Count

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:52.770
Dooku, but gets captured.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:57.160
After finding his mother dead and performing
a casual massacre, Anakin steals a droid and

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:59.420
goes with Padme to rescue Obi-Wan.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:02.970
They get captured, too, and are sent to an
arena to die with Obi-Wan.

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:03.970
"Good job!"

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:09.090
There’s some stuff about Jar-Jar causing
the downfall of society — “Meesa propose

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:15.870
that the senate give immediately emergency
powers to the Supreme Chancellor."

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:17.240
— goddammit, Jar Jar.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:22.700
Then, it’s your usual last 40 minute extravaganza
of lightsabers and explosions as Jedis and

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:24.530
the Clone Army battle the badmen.

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:29.610
It turns out the war was all because of the
dark side, as usual — "Everything is going

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:30.970
as planned."

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:35.140
— and Padme and Anakin get married despite
them both knowing it’s a bad idea.

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:36.690
"It would destroy us."

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:41.560
Episode II continues Lucas’ attempt to answer
the question: ‘How Does Democracy Slide

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:46.930
into Fascism?’And, to his credit, he does
this in a really smart way, by taking inspiration

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:49.500
from history and contemporary politics.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:53.490
The film opens with a bombing attempt to kill
Padme which sets all the events of the film

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:55.910
in motion and by the end has lead to war.

00:02:55.910 --> 00:03:00.060
"Begun the Clone War has."

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:04.160
Lucas seems to be invoking the assassination
of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, which is often

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:06.790
cited as the catalyst for World War I.

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:10.470
The film was released not even a year after
the 9/11 attacks that would then lead to the

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:11.810
invasion of Iraq.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:15.790
And here we have a blockbuster popcorn movie,
written before all that happened, providing

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:17.680
some prescient commentary.

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:21.650
Count Dooku and Darth Sidious manufacture
a conflict for their own ends: the threat

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:26.151
of Dooku’s droid army allows Palpatine to
gain emergency powers and control the clone

00:03:26.151 --> 00:03:27.151
army.

00:03:27.151 --> 00:03:32.100
Kinda like how Hitler was made Chancellor
of Germany in January of 1933: by manufacturing

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:36.450
conflict and a Bolshevik conspiracy against
the state, he was granted dictatorial powers

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.870
by the Reichstag in March of the same year.

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:41.510
And, well, we know how that turned out…

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:47.240
"Winter for Poland and France!"

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:51.310
The movie is also interested in showing how
choices made with even the best intentions

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:54.560
can be manipulated into benefiting the wrong
people.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:58.069
The Kaminoans were simply hired to create
an army, they didn’t know that the guy who

00:03:58.069 --> 00:04:00.150
placed the order was actually dead.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:03.560
“Master Sifo-Dias was killed almost ten
years ago.”

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:06.680
They also didn’t know that they were building
the stormtroopers that would eventually oppress

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:08.610
an entire galaxy.

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:12.709
Even poor old Jar Jar thinks he’s doing
what Padme would have wanted when he presents

00:04:12.709 --> 00:04:15.349
the motion to grant Palpatine emergency powers.

00:04:15.349 --> 00:04:19.070
“If only Senator Amidala were here…”

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:21.040
Again, Lucas is drawing from history.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:24.930
You can trace this kind of manipulation back
to events like the Munich Agreement.

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:29.040
At the time, this was considered a way of
preventing war by appeasing Hitler’s demand

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:30.600
for more territory.

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.350
Of course, it didn’t work.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:36.590
It was a deal struck with good intentions,
but turned out to be a major step towards

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:37.590
war.

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:39.190
Jar Jar Binks and Neville Chamberlain?

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:41.520
Ya played yourself.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:46.010
Now while this analysis of Socio-Political
history is going on, Lucas had the smart idea

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:50.450
of using a character drama at the centre of
the movie to highlight the personal side of

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:51.830
the larger conflict.

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:53.030
How does he do that?

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:55.110
He makes it a love story, of course!

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:57.610
"A love story against the backdrop of a war."

00:04:57.610 --> 00:05:01.720
Whether it’s Romeo and Juliet, Robin Hood
and Maid Marian, or Neo and Trinity, 'love

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:05.220
in a time of conflict’ is a story that’s
as old as drama itself.

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:10.560
But Lucas seems to have settled on one specific
movie influence for his doomed lovers: the

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:12.730
1965 romantic epic, Doctor Zhivago.

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:17.800
An adaptation of the Boris Pasternak novel,
Dr Zhivago is the story of doctor and poet

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:22.830
Yuri Zhivago whose life and love for a married
woman is dramatically changed by the conflicts

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:26.260
of the first world war, the Russian Revolution,
and the Russian Civil war.

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:32.040
It explores, in detail, the historical context
and puts him and his beloved Lara Antipova,

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.440
right at the centre of that conflict.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:35.440
Sound familiar?

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:39.690
From the themes to the posters which Lucas
specifically asked artist Drew Struzan to

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:42.360
imitate, the two movies have a lot in common.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:46.350
Both Zhivago and Anakin are taken from their
families at a young age and both bury their

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:47.910
mothers on screen.

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:50.880
Both stories are set at a time of burgeoning
civil war.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:54.830
Episode II shows the events leading up to
the Clone War, while Zhivago depicts the events

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:56.500
leading to the Russian Revolution.

00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:01.139
The romance central to both movies is forbidden
by social expectations or class.

00:06:01.139 --> 00:06:06.070
Anakin’s role as a Jedi forbids him from
love — “Attachment is forbidden” — whereas

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:10.860
Zhivago and Lara’s pre-existing marriages
hinder their love: Zhivago to the wealthy

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:14.270
Tonya, and Lara to the Bolshevik Commander
Strelnikov.

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:18.150
Both films even depict our main characters
forced to travel as refugees.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:22.870
The biggest similarity is in how both films
depict the devastating consequences ‘True

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:24.410
Love’ can have.

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:27.100
Love acts as a corrupting influence on Anakin.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:31.479
Whether it’s him disobeying orders to find
his mother and going psycho when she dies,

00:06:31.479 --> 00:06:35.540
or how he believes his and Padme’s love
could be hidden — "We could keep it a secret..."

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:40.290
— the strength of Anakin’s love causes
him to do some stupid, sinister and downright

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:41.610
evil things.

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:45.699
While it doesn’t go to the same extreme,
Doctor Zhivago depicts a good man who heals

00:06:45.699 --> 00:06:50.810
the sick, driven to breaking small laws like
stealing firewood to provide for his family,

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:54.669
then driven to lie and cheat on that family
because of his love for Lara.

00:06:54.669 --> 00:06:58.610
Zhivago’s love for Lara results in him abandoning
his wife and children.

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:03.370
But by the end, said love makes him abandon
Lara and his unborn child, as well, to save

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:04.660
them from persecution.

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:09.550
Anakin’s love for Padme, by contrast, results
in the death of thousands of Jedi and the

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:14.750
creation of a fascist Galactic Empire, which
seems a little like overkill to me.

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:18.710
So, if Attack of the Clones is cribbing so
closely from one of the great love stories

00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:21.370
of all time, why is it such a dud?

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:26.270
Sure, there’s the toe-curlingly bad dialogue
— "I hate sand." —&nbsp;and the total lack

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:30.430
of chemistry between Anakin and Padme, but
all of that might have been okay if it wasn’t

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:35.509
for the fact Lucas missed the mark on how
the politics and love story of Doctor Zhivago

00:07:35.509 --> 00:07:36.910
are integrated.

00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:40.880
Doctor Zhivago succeeds because the romantic
leads have conflicting loyalties, but are

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:45.389
willing to forgo them for the sake of love
and their shared compassion for others.

00:07:45.389 --> 00:07:49.630
Zhivago and Lara are seen together several
times in the first hour of the movie, but

00:07:49.630 --> 00:07:54.690
don’t actually come together until something
happens that reveals their shared the desire

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:55.690
to help the injured.

00:07:55.690 --> 00:07:56.800
"Are you a nurse?"

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:01.211
They, then, both end up working closely together
in a field hospital where, sure enough, they

00:08:01.211 --> 00:08:02.350
fall in love.

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:07.550
The fact Zhivago and Lara forego their respective
loyalties out of a desire to help, then fall

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:12.331
in love because of that, encapsulates how
well the political backdrop and love story

00:08:12.331 --> 00:08:13.500
are integrated.

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:18.259
Attack of the Clones, by contrast, pushes
Anakin and Padme together with forces outside

00:08:18.259 --> 00:08:19.370
of their control.

00:08:19.370 --> 00:08:24.060
Palpatine suggests Obi Wan and Anakin protect
Padme, then the Jedi Council orders Anakin

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:28.310
to protect Padme alone in the conveniently
romantic setting of Naboo.

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:30.870
None of these actions are their choice.

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:33.939
“Senator Amidala will not refuse an executive
order.”

00:08:33.939 --> 00:08:38.419
Padme and Anakin are forced together by the
political conflict, as opposed to being drawn

00:08:38.419 --> 00:08:41.899
to one another by their feelings about the
conflict.

00:08:41.899 --> 00:08:46.509
This is the same problem we mentioned in our
Episode I video: the characters are metaphorical

00:08:46.509 --> 00:08:52.369
‘Paper Boats’ being carried along by the
current of the plot; they have no agency.

00:08:52.369 --> 00:08:55.490
Whereas Zhivago’s arc is all about the choices
he makes.

00:08:55.490 --> 00:09:00.370
Near the beginning he is supportive of Bolshevik
protests — “Justice, equality, and bread.

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:02.930
Don't you think they're splendid?"

00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:08.040
“Yes, I do!” — and then sees their slaughter
in the square by the Cossacks.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:13.199
But later, his house is taken by the Communist
State — "There was living space for 13 families

00:09:13.199 --> 00:09:18.269
in this one house!" — he is forced to lie
about the existence of typhus and starvation

00:09:18.269 --> 00:09:21.300
in the capital — "You've been listening
to rumormongers, comrade.

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:25.940
There is no typhus in this city." — and
is even forced to flee with his family because

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:28.439
he now disagrees with the Party’s policies.

00:09:28.439 --> 00:09:33.279
Zhivago’s loyalties change over the course
of the movie and his choices reflect that.

00:09:33.279 --> 00:09:37.670
His choice to find Lara and be with her at
the end of the film is motivated by the events

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:38.670
of the film.

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:42.720
Instead of being railroaded into one way of
life by the changing political landscape,

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:44.740
he chooses to be with Lara.

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:46.329
No paper boats there.

00:09:46.329 --> 00:09:50.819
Episode II on the other hand offers no such
character arc or agency.

00:09:50.819 --> 00:09:55.050
Anakin starts as a disobedient brat — “And
you will pay attention to my lead.”

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:56.050
“Why?”

00:09:56.050 --> 00:09:59.920
"What?" — and ends as a disobedient brat
— “We'll take him together.

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:04.000
You go in slowly on the left, and-" "I'm taking
him now!"

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:05.019
"No, Anakin, no!

00:10:05.019 --> 00:10:06.019
No!"

00:10:06.019 --> 00:10:10.850
He starts off with an unrequited love for
Padme, and ends with a requited love because…

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:11.920
reasons?

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:16.369
We never hear his opinion on the conflict,
only his pretty suspect opinions on the running

00:10:16.369 --> 00:10:19.389
of the republic — “The- the trouble is
that people don't always agree."

00:10:19.389 --> 00:10:21.339
"Well, then they should be made to."

00:10:21.339 --> 00:10:24.170
"Sounds a lot like a dictatorship to me."

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:25.970
"Well, if it works..."

00:10:25.970 --> 00:10:28.759
And this isn’t just true of Zhivago and
Anakin.

00:10:28.759 --> 00:10:33.529
In Doctor Zhivago, Lara marries a Revolutionary,
but after being forced to flee those same

00:10:33.529 --> 00:10:37.959
revolutionaries she ends up saying, "This
is an awful time to be alive!"

00:10:37.959 --> 00:10:39.399
Her loyalties shift.

00:10:39.399 --> 00:10:44.189
Whereas Padme, believes in democracy at the
beginning of Star Wars and still does by the

00:10:44.189 --> 00:10:46.639
end, largely because the movie tells her to.

00:10:46.639 --> 00:10:50.629
“The day we stop believing democracy can
work is the day we lose it."

00:10:50.629 --> 00:10:52.619
"Let's pray that day never comes."

00:10:52.619 --> 00:10:56.959
The biggest change she undergoes is to confess
she loves Anakin after having resisted the

00:10:56.959 --> 00:10:58.459
relationship throughout the whole movie.

00:10:58.459 --> 00:10:59.670
“I shouldn’t have done that.”

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:05.519
“It'll take us to a place we cannot go,
regardless of the way we feel about each other.”

00:11:05.519 --> 00:11:10.040
And even then, that change of heart is only
because — "I think our lives are about to

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:11.040
be destroyed anyway."

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:12.040
How romantic.

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:16.139
While Padme shows some signs of resistance,
— "I do not like this idea of hiding."

00:11:16.139 --> 00:11:21.079
— she still shows little agency in her decisions,
if she makes any at all.

00:11:21.079 --> 00:11:25.560
But surely, in a movie that ends with war,
the politics must have some bearing on the

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:27.329
character arcs, right?

00:11:27.329 --> 00:11:29.009
Well, not really…

00:11:29.009 --> 00:11:33.550
Doctor Zhivago succeeds in exploring the complexities
of relationships at a time of war because

00:11:33.550 --> 00:11:36.480
it shows us the moral ambiguity of that time.

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:41.610
The whole first hour is about setting up Oligarchs
like Lara’s employer to be heartless monsters,

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:46.899
only for the idealistic revolutionaries like
Lara’s husband to become equally heartless.

00:11:46.899 --> 00:11:50.429
Lara and Zhivago are caught in the middle
of these blurred distinctions.

00:11:50.429 --> 00:11:55.279
Lara dines and dances with her creepy, oligarch
employer Komarovsky at a fancy club, but when

00:11:55.279 --> 00:12:01.119
she meets up with her wide-eyed idealist husband-to-be,
Pasha, a few scenes later, he says this is

00:12:01.119 --> 00:12:05.740
— “Where the pigs were eating and dancing!”
—&nbsp;not knowing Lara was there.

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:09.850
At the end of the movie, Zhivago must accept
Komarovsky’s help to save Lara — "These

00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:11.749
men that came with me today, as an escort,
will come for her and the child tomorrow,

00:12:11.749 --> 00:12:19.189
as a firing squad! — a man who earlier raped
Lara and then was shot by her, a wound Zhivago

00:12:19.189 --> 00:12:20.639
is asked to heal.

00:12:20.639 --> 00:12:25.089
These are the kind of complexities and confused
loyalties that life in a time of conflict

00:12:25.089 --> 00:12:28.949
creates; complexities that don’t really
exist in Attack of the Clones.

00:12:28.949 --> 00:12:34.619
For example, where Dr. Zhivago has the character
of Pasha, a political protester turned tyrannical

00:12:34.619 --> 00:12:38.589
commander, Attack of the Clones has Count
Dooku, a former Jedi turned traitor to the

00:12:38.589 --> 00:12:39.589
Republic.

00:12:39.589 --> 00:12:42.070
Both characters are depicted in similar ways.

00:12:42.070 --> 00:12:46.829
Pasha, who becomes the commander of the Red
Army, changes his name to ‘Strelnikov’,

00:12:46.829 --> 00:12:49.220
and Count Dooku changes his name to Tyranus.

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:53.709
They are both either shown or explained to
be idealists at the start.

00:12:53.709 --> 00:12:57.490
Dooku is referred to within the first five
minutes of the movie as, — “He's a political

00:12:57.490 --> 00:12:59.449
idealist, not a murderer."

00:12:59.449 --> 00:13:03.249
“You know, my lady, Count Dooku was once
a jedi.

00:13:03.249 --> 00:13:06.619
He couldn't assassinate anyone; it’s not
in his character.”

00:13:06.619 --> 00:13:10.870
— while we are introduced to Pasha handing
out fliers to workers for a protest.

00:13:10.870 --> 00:13:14.860
The problem is how we see these characters
change and develop over the course of the

00:13:14.860 --> 00:13:15.860
movie.

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:20.649
We think Pasha is killed in the trenches of
WWI only for his reveal as Strelnikov to be

00:13:20.649 --> 00:13:23.739
the shock twist that concludes the first half
of the movie.

00:13:23.739 --> 00:13:27.619
We knew his allegiances before the war, and
it makes sense that he would make his way

00:13:27.619 --> 00:13:29.309
up the ranks of the Bolshevik army.

00:13:29.309 --> 00:13:33.889
It’s a twist because we thought he was dead
not because his motivations changed.

00:13:33.889 --> 00:13:37.749
There is no revelation or even catharsis in
Attack of the Clones.

00:13:37.749 --> 00:13:41.649
The next time we hear anything about Dooku,
after his passing mention at the start, is

00:13:41.649 --> 00:13:46.769
over halfway through when he appears and basically
explains his entire conspiracy while Obi Wan

00:13:46.769 --> 00:13:47.889
is in earshot.

00:13:47.889 --> 00:13:51.730
After that, he tries to convince Obi Wan to
join his cause by pretending to be on his

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:57.850
side — “Oh no, my friend, this is a mistake,
a terrible mistake they have gone too far!

00:13:57.850 --> 00:13:58.850
This is madness!”

00:13:58.850 --> 00:14:00.970
This would have been a great opportunity to
develop his character more.

00:14:00.970 --> 00:14:05.239
If he had been introduced earlier, it could
show some conflict about his ideals, maybe

00:14:05.239 --> 00:14:07.679
then Obi Wan would sympathize with him.

00:14:07.679 --> 00:14:12.269
But no, the next minute Dooku sentences them
all to death, chops off Anakin’s arm and

00:14:12.269 --> 00:14:14.949
delivers the Death Star plans to Darth Sidious.

00:14:14.949 --> 00:14:16.959
No such moral ambiguity there.

00:14:16.959 --> 00:14:21.269
Other than a few sentences from nameless characters
at the start, the audience is given no reason

00:14:21.269 --> 00:14:24.709
to care about Dooku’s treachery or where
his loyalties lie.

00:14:24.709 --> 00:14:29.189
A lot of these problems can be traced back
to the same script issues Episode I had.

00:14:29.189 --> 00:14:33.059
Whether it’s the threat of Padme’s life,
being sent to Naboo, Obi Wan stumbling across

00:14:33.059 --> 00:14:38.480
the Clone Army, or a declaration of war, all
of this happens to the characters, not because

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:39.839
of the characters.

00:14:39.839 --> 00:14:44.100
In fact, the most significant decision of
the whole movie is done off screen by Jar

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:47.649
Jar, who decides to grant the Chancellor executive
powers.

00:14:47.649 --> 00:14:51.889
This choice shapes the fate of the galaxy,
but the most we see of his decision-making

00:14:51.889 --> 00:14:54.389
process is a shot of him looking pensive.

00:14:54.389 --> 00:14:57.639
Episode II’s biggest flaw, though, is the
romance itself.

00:14:57.639 --> 00:15:02.820
Where Zhivago and Lara’s love is tortured
and forbidden, but ultimately feels true,

00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:07.119
Anakin and Padme’s is meant to be all those
things but we’re never really given any

00:15:07.119 --> 00:15:08.619
reason to believe that.

00:15:08.619 --> 00:15:14.230
Zhivago falls for a woman who shares his compassion,
while Lara falls for a good man of great talent

00:15:14.230 --> 00:15:15.980
and character whom she admires.

00:15:15.980 --> 00:15:21.069
Meanwhile, across the galaxy, a moody teenager
inexplicably wins over a much older woman

00:15:21.069 --> 00:15:23.600
by being really — “My goodness you've
grown."

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:24.929
"So have you...

00:15:24.929 --> 00:15:27.160
grown more beautiful, I mean."

00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:29.569
— really — “I don't think she liked
me watching her."

00:15:29.569 --> 00:15:32.220
— really — “The thought of not being
with you...

00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:34.319
I can't breathe."

00:15:34.319 --> 00:15:35.720
—&nbsp;REALLY creepy.

00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:40.910
Not only that, the ‘forbidden’ nature
of their romance doesn’t seem all that forbidden.

00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:44.919
Though we are repeatedly told it’s wrong
— "You're studying to become a jedi, I'm-

00:15:44.919 --> 00:15:45.989
I'm a senator."

00:15:45.989 --> 00:15:48.879
— we’re not really shown any drawbacks.

00:15:48.879 --> 00:15:54.600
Anakin even explains a loophole — “Compassion,
which I would define as unconditional love,

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:58.689
is central to a Jedi’s life.

00:15:58.689 --> 00:16:05.079
So, you might say that we are encouraged to
love.” — and though Obi Wan says Anakin

00:16:05.079 --> 00:16:09.319
has made a commitment to the Jedi Order, he
also says, “You have made a commitment to

00:16:09.319 --> 00:16:11.799
the Jedi order, a commitment not easily broken."

00:16:11.799 --> 00:16:15.619
So wait, it’s not easy but CAN be broken?

00:16:15.619 --> 00:16:19.990
That, and Anakin and Padme don’t seem all
that shy about hiding it when they make out

00:16:19.990 --> 00:16:23.519
in front of an ARENA FULL OF PEOPLE, INCLUDING
OBI WAN.

00:16:23.519 --> 00:16:25.869
Their relationship is discouraged, but not
‘forbidden.'

00:16:25.869 --> 00:16:30.009
And they don’t seem to love each other because
they admire one another’s characters, political

00:16:30.009 --> 00:16:34.879
beliefs or talents like Zhivago and Lara,
they love each other because the script tells

00:16:34.879 --> 00:16:35.919
them to.

00:16:35.919 --> 00:16:39.129
In the end, Attack of the Clones fails because
of its ambitions.

00:16:39.129 --> 00:16:44.269
By trying to make the movie a cautionary political
drama, an Epic Romance, a conspiracy thriller,

00:16:44.269 --> 00:16:49.750
a mystery, and a war movie, Lucas gives himself
too much ground to cover in two hours, and

00:16:49.750 --> 00:16:53.639
as a result, he doesn’t integrate these
pieces well enough to give them time enough

00:16:53.639 --> 00:16:54.720
to develop.

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:59.500
Time that Doctor Zhivago has in spades with
its 3 hours and 20 minute running time.

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:04.230
And while taking some surface pointers from
a cinema classic is admirable, it fails to

00:17:04.230 --> 00:17:07.390
deliver on all those key elements that made
the original so great.

00:17:07.390 --> 00:17:08.890
“Alright, I get the picture."

00:17:08.890 --> 00:17:10.170
But what do you think Wisecrack?

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:15.180
Is it a misunderstood masterpiece, or do you
hate it as much as Anakin hates sand?

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:18.610
Let us know in the comments, and thanks to
all our patrons who support the channel and

00:17:18.610 --> 00:17:19.780
our podcasts.

00:17:19.780 --> 00:18:17.420
Be sure to hit that subscribe button.

